The preceding records now form a joint discharge package.  The
conditional theorem \Cref{thm:ns:framework-deliverable-regularity}
reads the same package as hypotheses implying regularity.  The
AOR-instance theorem reads the package as the membership witness for
\(\mathsf C_{\mathrm{NS}}\) in the refinement-stable AOR class.

\begin{theorem}[NS smooth-data carrier in $\mathbf{AOR}^{\mathrm{ref}}$]\label{thm:ns:in-refstableaor}
Under the discharges of \Cref{thm:ns:aor-mechanical-r-records}, \Cref{thm:ns:aor-r4-discharge}, \Cref{thm:ns:aor-r6-discharge}, and \Cref{thm:ns:aor-r7-discharge}, together with the quotient-transport record of \Cref{thm:ns:aor-quotient-transport-record}, the Navier--Stokes smooth-data carrier $\mathsf C_{\mathrm{NS}}$ belongs to $\mathbf{AOR}^{\mathrm{ref}}_{\mathfrak S_{\mathrm{NS}}}$.  By the AOR meta-theory's discipline, audit-closure membership gives the record-level AOR reading of the regularity conclusion; the framework-deliverable regularity statement \Cref{thm:ns:framework-deliverable-regularity} is the same content read in conditional form, with the closure stack presented as hypotheses rather than as the membership witness.
\end{theorem}

